/// Records that a citizen has read an item (a subject), e.g. a task, an event
/// or a changelog entry. An item without a marker of the citizen is unread;
/// the rule of its subject type decides whether an unread item is "new".
///
/// Each subject type has its own nullable column. A subject that is a
/// database row has a foreign key, thus the database keeps the integrity and
/// deletes the markers together with their item. A changelog entry is code
/// in the app, thus its column holds the key of the entry without a foreign
/// key. A hand-written CHECK constraint (see the migrations `read_markers`
/// and `changelog_read_markers`) makes sure that exactly one of these columns
/// is set.
///
/// To add a subject type in the database: add a nullable column (with its
/// relation named `readMarkers` on the subject model, when the subject is a
/// database row) and a unique constraint of the column together with
/// `citizenId`, and extend the CHECK constraint in a new migration. The steps
/// in the code are documented at `READ_MARKER_SUBJECT_RULES` in
/// `packages/application/src/readMarkers/readMarkerSubjects.ts`.
model ReadMarker {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid(2))
  citizenId String
  citizen   Citizen  @relation(fields: [citizenId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  readAt    DateTime @default(now()) @db.Timestamptz(3)

  taskId  String?
  task    Task?   @relation(fields: [taskId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  eventId String?
  event   Event?  @relation(fields: [eventId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  changelogEntryKey String?

  /// The subject column comes first, thus the same index also serves the
  /// cascade delete of the subject.
  @@unique([taskId, citizenId])
  @@unique([eventId, citizenId])
  @@unique([changelogEntryKey, citizenId])
  @@index([citizenId])
}
